<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tervel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 700–721</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Khan Tervel · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dulo-clan">Dulo clan</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tervel was khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> from about 700 to about 721, the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparuh</a>. In 705 he helped the deposed emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-ii">Justinian II</a> regain the Byzantine throne, receiving the title of caesar and a territory south of the Balkan mountains. In 717–718 he sent forces that helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> break the Arab siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Tervel is first mentioned in Byzantine sources in 704, when the deposed and exiled emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-ii">Justinian II</a> approached him. Justinian secured his support for a restoration in exchange for friendship, gifts and his daughter in marriage. With 15,000 horsemen provided by Tervel, Justinian entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 705 and executed the emperors Leontius and Tiberius III. Tervel received gifts, the title of kaisar, which made him second only to the emperor and the first foreign ruler to hold it, and a concession in northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> called Zagora. Whether Justinian's daughter Anastasia was in fact married to Tervel is unknown.</p><p>Three years later Justinian broke the arrangement and moved to recover the area, but Tervel routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomorie">Anchialus</a> in 708. In 711 Justinian asked again for help and received only 3,000 men; he was captured and executed, and Tervel raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> in 712, plundering as far as the neighbourhood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> under Maslama ibn Abd al-Malik besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 717, the new emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-iii-the-isaurian">Leo III the Isaurian</a> appealed to Tervel on the strength of a treaty of 716. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> attacked the Arab rear and, in the early summer of 718, defeated them in battle; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophanes-the-confessor">Theophanes the Confessor</a> says they killed some 22,000. Shortly afterwards the Arabs raised the siege.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Nominalia of the Bulgarian khans places Tervel in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dulo-clan">Dulo clan</a> and gives him a reign of 21 years. Most researchers think the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madara-rider">Madara Rider</a> relief was made in his time to commemorate victories over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and to honour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparukh</a>. A later cult of a St Trivelius rests on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paisius-of-hilendar">Paisius of Hilendar</a>'s History of 1762, which identified him with a monk shown in an engraving of 1741; the engraving in fact depicted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dragutin">Stefan Dragutin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> as Saint Theoctistus. Tervel Peak on Livingston Island in Antarctica is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tervel of Bulgaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Tervel</b> helped the deposed emperor Justinian II regain his throne in 705 and in 717–718 sent forces that assisted Byzantium against the Arab siege of Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
